{
  "image_name": "4e7ce6301950fd8b2e7fb2491d46d57a.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes a developmental map of blood and immune cell lineages starting from hematopoietic stem cells and multipotent progenitors at the top, then branching into lymphoid, dendritic-cell, and monocyte/macrophage pathways below. The top portion shows early progenitors such as MPP2–4, CMP, GMP, and MDP, with arrows and transcription factors indicating how fate choices are regulated.\n\nThe central message is the relationship between dendritic cells and nearby myeloid or lymphoid branches. A common dendritic progenitor (CDP) gives rise to several precursor states, including pre-pDC, pre-cDC1, and pre-cDC2, which then mature into pDC, cDC1, and cDC2 subsets. Distinct factors such as Irf8, Batf3, Notch2, Klf4, Tcf4, and Zeb2 appear to steer these transitions. Colored boxes group these populations into broader regulatory modules marked by labels such as Irf8-high, Zbtb46-positive, Irf4-high, Mafb-positive, and Msa4a3-positive, emphasizing overlaps and separations between dendritic and monocyte-related identities.\n\nTo the right, GMP-derived pathways produce granulocyte progenitors and monocyte progenitors, leading to monocytes, macrophages, and inflammatory monocyte-derived cells. Overall, the figure highlights that immune cell differentiation is a branching continuum controlled by specific transcriptional programs.",
